1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:24,157
Commander Pike offered me a patrol car. A regular patrol car. Nigga
had watched me turn a Lamborghini into a rescue platform and decided
what I needed next was a sedan with enough trunk space for cones. I
looked out his window at my Lamborghini waiting below. He looked too.
That was a stupid offer, he said. Not stupid. Hopeful.

2
00:00:24,407 --> 00:00:43,221
Tank looked at the patrol car through the window. That nigga got cloth
seats. Pike cleared his throat. They're durable. Nigga, so is my
aunt's couch. I ain't chasing nobody in it. Your aunt got a very
aggressive couch, I reminded him. That's different. That nigga been
through things.

3
00:00:43,471 --> 00:01:06,508
Pike looked between us, trying to decide whether he had hired two
idiots or discovered a form of policing his academy had failed to
teach. Then he glanced at my gold badge and chose faith. Campus patrol
occupied a stone building between a bakery and a store selling capes.
The bakery smelled better than any police station I had visited.

4
00:01:06,758 --> 00:01:32,424
The cape store had a line outside. People apparently bought new
identities the way niggas in Vegas bought belts. Pike spread a map
over his desk. The academy occupied the largest island. Six smaller
districts floated around it, joined by marked lanes sustained by the
school's field. If you stayed on those lanes with the right receivers,
your car could drive through open air.

5
00:01:32,674 --> 00:01:57,635
Leave the field and gravity remembered your ass immediately. The
largest satellite was called the Crown District. Hotels, restaurants,
showrooms, a marina for airships, and the Cloud Nine casino. Finn
Cinder's car had headed there after leaving the academy. Can your
people follow him? I asked. They can. He knows our cars. I turned my
badge over in my hand.

6
00:01:57,885 --> 00:02:18,018
Then let's introduce him to mine. Tank drove with me. Victor asked to
come, and I told him there was no third seat. I can fly. Nigga, why
you asking for a ride? He looked at Pike. Pike found a fascinating
place on his map to point at until Victor left. Shawna had plans of
her own.

7
00:02:18,268 --> 00:02:44,189
The dean had invited her and Rosa to rehearse with the academy's
evening orchestra. My wife had heard orchestra and doors ordinarily
locked to visitors in the same sentence and decided music deserved her
immediate attention. Mercedes went with Petra to see the lower
training gardens. Porsche announced she was going shopping. She said
it with such innocence I gave her a second look. What?

8
00:02:44,439 --> 00:03:10,185
she asked. Nothing. Exactly. Mind your police business. That woman
could sell rain to the clouds and make them apologize for being dry.
We left the patrol station under a bright afternoon sun. The
Lamborghini rolled between white pillars and onto the first sky lane.
Blue strips of light ran beneath the tires. Beyond either side was
nothing but air. Tank stared out his window.

9
00:03:10,435 --> 00:03:34,719
I miss curbs. You used to hit every curb in Vegas. That's how I knew
they was there. He unfolded Pike's map across his knees. It covered
the glove compartment, his right hand, and a substantial amount of
faith. Nigga, why the map got a back? Roads below roads. That's
stacking problems. Victor's voice came through the radio. Take the
next left. Tank looked up.

10
00:03:34,969 --> 00:03:57,350
Victor floated above the lane with the leisure of a nigga whose
vehicle was himself. Which next left? Tank asked. The turn beneath me.
Nigga, everything is beneath you. That's the whole issue. I laughed.
Tank didn't. He flattened the map against the dash and found the blue
line with his thumb. We need Crown. This left goes back to the
stadium.

11
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:20,962
There was a short silence. The following left, Victor said. Look at
that. This nigga just invented the next next left. Thank you, Tank. My
brother paused, pleased despite himself, then ruined the humility by
nodding at me. You heard the airborne nigga. Valuable team member.
You've been valuable. Say it with directional respect. I took his
turn.

12
00:04:21,212 --> 00:04:45,807
The lane curved beneath a stone bridge and the Crown District opened
ahead, windows stacked in sunlight. Tank folded the map down to one
manageable square. I got you, he said. I know. He settled back and
adjusted the vent toward his face. The air smelled faintly of the
bakery we'd left behind. My brother looked almost comfortable. Then
the lane dipped.

13
00:04:46,057 --> 00:05:10,805
He caught the door handle with his whole hand. Nigga, road got a
basement. You just explained roads below roads. On paper. I approved
that shit on paper. Victor laughed over the radio, finally too
entertained to pretend he was above us in any useful sense. Tank
pointed upward through the windshield. Don't laugh, flying nigga. You
still on navigation probation.

14
00:05:11,055 --> 00:05:35,981
Ahead of us the Crown District glittered. A red sports car passed on
the other side of the lane, driven by a man with gold skin and a woman
wearing a hat shaped like a small thunderstorm. Rain fell exclusively
into her champagne glass. Tank watched them go. Rosa gonna want that.
The hat? The champagne weather. We spotted Finn outside a showroom
called ASCENT.

15
00:05:36,231 --> 00:05:59,686
He stood between a black coupe and a salesman whose smile looked
physically painful. Two guards waited near the door. None wore masks
now. Finn had a strip of tape over the eyebrow I had helped him lose.
I parked across the road. He saw the Lamborghini before he saw me.
That gave him half a second to enjoy life. Then I stepped out. Finn.

16
00:05:59,936 --> 00:06:26,213
His face changed. The salesman looked relieved someone else had become
his problem. Captain, Finn said. We keep meeting. That's because
niggas keep letting you go. He looked up. Victor floated above the
showroom roof, arms folded, electricity crawling over his shoulders.
Finn smiled at him. You get your bracelet off? Victor descended six
feet. I raised a hand. I got this. Finn's guards shifted.

17
00:06:26,463 --> 00:06:48,462
Tank leaned against the Lamborghini and opened his jacket just enough
for them to understand the local temperature. Y'all can keep standing,
he said. Just stand peaceful. I approached Finn. Who paid you to hit
the hall? You're assuming money. Nigga, your shoes cost six thousand
dollars and you look allergic to principles. I'm assuming correctly.

18
00:06:48,712 --> 00:07:12,106
The salesman made a little noise that might have been laughter and
immediately pretended to cough. Finn's eyes went flat. You don't know
the city you're in. Then show me. He did. The black coupe leaped
backward through the showroom window. Glass burst into the street.
Finn dove through the open passenger door as the car spun. One guard
threw a smoke sphere.

19
00:07:12,356 --> 00:07:35,108
Tank caught it, looked offended, and threw it back. The sphere burst
under the man's feet. His first cough sounded like regret. The coupe
shot onto the sky lane. I was in the Lamborghini before the broken
glass finished falling. Tank landed beside me. Nigga, we arresting him
or racing him? He picking the format. I hit the siren.

20
00:07:35,358 --> 00:07:57,262
Blue lights lit the clouds beneath us. The Lamborghini lunged forward.
Finn's car swerved around a delivery van and accelerated toward the
Crown District's outer loop. I followed. The road curved around a
hotel whose swimming pool hung over open air. People at the pool stood
up when we passed. A woman waved.

21
00:07:57,512 --> 00:08:20,705
Her companion stared at the police lights and spilled an entire tray
of drinks into his own lap. Victor flew above us, keeping pace. He has
two more vehicles joining, he said through the patrol radio Pike had
given me. Where? Two silver cars came out of a side lane. There.
Useful timing, nigga. The first silver car struck my rear quarter.

22
00:08:20,955 --> 00:08:42,322
The Lamborghini slid toward the lane's luminous edge. For one second
Tank's window showed nothing below us at all. He turned to me very
calmly. Aiden. I know. I steered into the slide, let the rear swing,
and put us back between the blue strips. The silver car tried again.
This time I braked.

23
00:08:42,572 --> 00:09:04,486
It shot past, clipped Finn's coupe, and bounced toward the outer
barrier. Victor caught it with a web of crackling light. The driver
stared through his windshield at the man holding his car over the
clouds. Victor smiled without warmth. Wait here. He set the car on a
maintenance bay and welded its doors shut with a bright hiss.

24
00:09:04,736 --> 00:09:28,969
It wasn't going anywhere until somebody with tools and a better
attitude arrived. The second silver car pulled alongside us. A gun
came through its window. Tank leaned back as a shot shattered my
mirror. Nigga shot the mirror. I saw. That's how we see other niggas.
He returned fire. The attacking car veered off, sparks skipping from
its door.

25
00:09:29,219 --> 00:09:53,050
I drove between it and a tourist coach full of adults in matching
yellow jackets. The coach passengers flattened themselves against the
opposite windows. One of them gave me a thumbs-up. I gave it back.
Customer service did not stop because somebody was trying to kill me.
Finn headed for a spiral lane climbing around a hotel tower. I
followed him up. Faster.

26
00:09:53,300 --> 00:10:16,988
The engine climbed toward its beautiful, furious scream. Wind punched
through the broken mirror housing. Tank laughed the way he always
laughed when terror finally realized it had come to the wrong man. We
burst onto the hotel's roof. A wedding reception was taking place
there. White chairs. Gold glasses. A fountain full of flowers.

27
00:10:17,238 --> 00:10:41,430
Two adult grooms stood beneath an arch, both in immaculate suits, both
looking at Finn's car as it tore between their guests. He knocked over
the cake. I will admit that made it personal in a new way. Hold tight.
Nigga, to what? I drove onto the narrow stone rim around the fountain.
The Lamborghini rose sideways. Water flashed beneath Tank's door.

28
00:10:41,680 --> 00:11:03,082
We passed the fallen cake on two wheels, cleared a table, and landed
in front of the arch. Both grooms stared. Congratulations, I shouted.
Then we were gone. Behind us the wedding guests began cheering. Ahead,
Finn took the departure ramp toward the smaller northern island. The
black coupe's rear lights flickered.

29
00:11:03,332 --> 00:11:30,437
Something unfolded from its sides: short angular wings, illegal enough
that the patrol radio immediately began shouting about them. He's
leaving the lane, Pike warned. Finn's car lifted. My car did not have
wings. Bell had been very clear about that. Tank turned his head
slowly toward me. Remember what the four-arm nigga said. I remember.
You got the remembering face and the doing-it-anyway face.

30
00:11:30,687 --> 00:11:56,526
Right now they fighting. I watched the coupe rise over the northern
island's cliff. Beneath it a service lane curved through the rock,
then emerged on the far side beside a loading platform. I took the
service lane. Darkness swallowed us. The engine thundered against
stone. Ahead, daylight shrank to a small white rectangle. Tank braced
both feet. We came out under Finn's car.

31
00:11:56,776 --> 00:12:20,940
Not flying. Driving up the last steep piece of lane at exactly the
moment he drifted over it. The Lamborghini's roof struck the underside
of his wing. The wing snapped backward. The black coupe rolled,
skidded across the loading platform, and hit a stack of empty crates
with a crash that sent wood flying all the way to a restaurant
balcony. I stopped beside it.

32
00:12:21,190 --> 00:12:44,359
The Lamborghini's hood smoked. Tank stared at me. Nigga. What? You
jumped a car without jumping your car. Efficient. He began laughing so
hard he had to lean against the door before he got out. Finn crawled
from the coupe. His coat was torn. His expensive shoes were covered in
champagne-colored dust from somebody's broken shipment.

33
00:12:44,609 --> 00:13:06,336
He looked at my car with something dangerously close to reverence. You
cannot be human. I showed him the badge. I'm employed. He reached
toward his belt. Tank drew. Finn stopped reaching. Then the loading
platform's doors opened behind him. Voss's guards stepped out. Six of
them. Weapons lowered but ready.

34
00:13:06,586 --> 00:13:30,330
At their center stood a woman with close-cropped red hair and a black
uniform stitched with the downward wing. She looked about forty. Her
voice could have broken a glass without raising its volume. This is
restricted academy property. Good, I said. Then you'll have cameras.
Hand over the prisoner. Funny. That's how this morning started. Victor
landed beside me.

35
00:13:30,580 --> 00:13:55,663
Blue light ran along his fingers. The guards looked at him and
adjusted their expectations. Captain Aiden made this arrest, Victor
said. The woman studied him. Mr. Voltage. Your scholarship review is
next week. He went still. I felt it: the invisible leash she had
expected to tighten around his throat. I stepped in front of him.
Nigga, I didn't ask about his scholarship.

36
00:13:55,913 --> 00:14:19,589
She looked back at me. Do you understand where you are? A loading
platform with six guns and too much attitude. Pike's patrol cars
arrived behind us. Eight officers got out. The gray-bearded commander
walked to my side and held out a pair of cuffs. I put them on Finn
myself. For the first time all afternoon, nobody had a clever thing to
say.

37
00:14:19,840 --> 00:14:44,420
We left with the prisoner. The woman watched until the sky lane curved
away. Finn sat in the back of Pike's car. I followed in the battered
Lamborghini with Victor overhead. Tank kept turning around to make
sure no black uniforms had developed an urgent need to explain
themselves with bullets. My phone rang. Shawna. Baby, I might have
scratched the car, I said.

38
00:14:44,670 --> 00:15:08,438
I know. Your wedding stunt already on six phones in this room. They
happy? One groom wants you to sign his jacket. Tell him yes. Her voice
lowered. Aiden. We found something. I listened. She and Rosa had been
rehearsing in the old music hall. While the orchestra took a break,
Rosa heard a voice coming through a brass ventilation grille beneath
the stage.

39
00:15:08,688 --> 00:15:31,481
Not talking. Singing. The same four lines over and over, in a woman's
tired but steady voice. Shawna knelt beside the grille and sang the
next line. The voice stopped. Then somebody said, very clearly, Are
you above us? My grip tightened on the wheel. Shawna asked who was
there. The woman said her name was Lena March. Thirty-two.

40
00:15:31,731 --> 00:15:48,776
A singer who could carry her voice through solid matter if she knew
where to aim it. She had been in the lower annex for five months. Her
release date changed every time she reached it. She says there are
people down there who aren't on any student list, Shawna told me.

41
00:15:49,026 --> 00:16:09,299
She says they take them through a round door, and some come back old.
Some don't come back at all. Ahead of me Finn looked through the rear
window of Pike's car. Our eyes met. The little grin was gone. What
else? I asked. She heard the name Winter Engine. And there's a doctor
down there. Reiss.

42
00:16:09,549 --> 00:16:32,457
He told her if she ever found somebody outside, she had to say the
real heart isn't in the hall. I looked up at the academy floating
above the sunlit clouds. Baby, stay with Rosa. Tell Storm only. Nobody
in black. I'm not leaving that woman singing into a wall. I know. You
know what that means? Means we got plans tonight.

43
00:16:32,707 --> 00:16:56,831
Her breath softened in my ear. Come back first. Always. When we
reached the station, Finn asked to speak to me without Voss's people
present. He sat in a cell with ordinary iron bars, which seemed almost
embarrassingly old-fashioned after everything we had driven through. I
stood outside with Pike and Victor. Finn rested his elbows on his
knees.

44
00:16:57,081 --> 00:17:17,837
You think the bottom of this school is the bottom, he said. Explain.
He looked at the ceiling. There's a door under the prison. It doesn't
open into another room. Victor's face tightened. Where does it open?
Finn swallowed. Another year. The bakery next door rang its little
bell. Somebody laughed out on the sidewalk.

45
00:17:18,087 --> 00:17:39,691
Through Pike's window I could see my car shining in the sun, every
dent earned in front of witnesses. For a moment those ordinary sounds
felt far away. Then I leaned toward the bars. Nigga, I said, you
better start at the beginning. He began with a car, which was the
first sensible thing the man had done all day.

46
00:17:39,941 --> 00:17:59,541
Three months earlier, Voss's red-haired security commander had hired
him to transport a sealed engine housing from the Crown District to
the lower annex. Her name was Commander Sable. She paid well, asked no
questions about his history, and made it clear she expected the same
courtesy.

47
00:17:59,791 --> 00:18:24,223
Finn had driven the housing down the service ramp at midnight and
waited while two technicians unloaded it beside a circular metal
frame. The room smelled like cold iron, he said. There were rails
going straight through the circle. Not our kind of rails. Old ones.
And a clock over it with three hands. Victor folded his arms. A
chronal aperture. Finn looked at him.

48
00:18:24,473 --> 00:18:46,743
Whatever the fuck you call it, there was a field on the other side.
Real grass. A little station building. Snow on the roof. I could feel
cold coming through. You went through? I asked. No. A man came out. He
rubbed his wrists where the cuffs had been. The man had worn a long
military coat and carried a leather case.

49
00:18:46,993 --> 00:19:08,563
Two others followed him, pushing a cart with a covered shape on it.
Finn thought it was equipment until the covering moved. He heard a
person beneath it ask for water. Sable told him to face the wall. He
faced it. When he turned around, the strangers were gone. The cart
remained. A dark stain spread across its blanket.

50
00:19:08,813 --> 00:19:32,395
Not blood, Finn insisted; something like blue ink that glowed on the
edges. They'd taken somebody's power too far, Victor said quietly.
Finn wouldn't meet his eyes. I watched the man's face. The swagger
kept trying to come back, finding no place to stand, and falling away
again. And you went back to work for them. I saw a thing I didn't
understand.

51
00:19:32,645 --> 00:19:51,730
I've seen a lot of things I didn't understand. You understood water.
He went silent. I let him sit in that silence. I didn't need a speech
from him. I needed the part he hadn't yet decided to tell us.
Eventually he looked at Pike. There's a race tonight. The Sundown
Circuit.

52
00:19:51,980 --> 00:20:15,231
Sable moves the lower-annex manifests during the race because half
your patrol is managing the spectators. She carries them herself, in a
silver case. If you want names, that case has names. Victor stared at
him. The race was scheduled last month. Yeah. And I'm telling you why
your chancellor suddenly agreed to sponsor it. I looked at Pike.

53
00:20:15,481 --> 00:20:39,816
He had already reached for his phone. No, I said. We tell Storm in
person. Pike stopped. And your next meal, I told Finn, comes after you
give the commander every route you remember. Nobody asking you to be
beautiful. Just useful. Outside, late afternoon had softened the
towers to gold. Victor walked with me toward the Lamborghini without
saying anything.

54
00:20:40,066 --> 00:21:03,199
He stopped by the repaired front fender and put his hand on it as if
touching an animal he hadn't decided whether to trust. I'm entered in
that race, he said. You drive? Very well. I waited. He looked at me. I
know how that sounded. Nigga, I'm letting you enjoy it. He almost
smiled. There's another entry available. Bell prepared a demonstration
car.

55
00:21:03,449 --> 00:21:23,726
It needs a driver. I glanced at my Lamborghini, then up at the circuit
winding around the highest towers. The setting sun caught the lanes
and turned them into long strips of fire. Somewhere under us a woman
was singing because it was the only way she could reach the world.
Tell Bell I got a car, I said.

56
00:21:23,976 --> 00:21:35,897
Tell him what I need is a number. Victor nodded once. Which number? I
looked at him until the answer arrived in his face. One, he said. See,
nigga? We learning together.
